WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.467 --> 00:00:02.966
You guys strike me as a Mutt and Jeff team.

00:00:02.967 --> 00:00:05.266
And so that must have been --

00:00:05.267 --> 00:00:06.332
Elvis and Costello.

00:00:06.333 --> 00:00:09.632
An Elvis and Costello team maybe?

00:00:09.633 --> 00:00:13.232
So you know, what did you guys see in each other?

00:00:13.233 --> 00:00:15.999
How did it work? Were there times when you guys couldn't

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:19.732
see eye to eye? You know, it's a little bit like a marriage, right?

00:00:19.733 --> 00:00:23.432
We fought in the lab like cats and dogs. People said they didn't want to

00:00:23.433 --> 00:00:25.299
be around us. Like it was being around a married couple

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:27.099
where we were getting ready to get a divorce.

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:31.199
No, they literally, people would leave the lab when the two of us were working together.

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:38.199
But the fact is, when you have a collaboration that's as close as John

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:43.899
and as effective as John, part of the reason for that is because

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:49.499
there's a complimentary compensation of each other's weaknesses.

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:52.132
Oh really?

00:00:52.133 --> 00:00:54.132
Do either of us have any?

00:00:54.133 --> 00:00:56.166
That's the point!

00:00:56.167 --> 00:00:59.932
I mean, I'm the messiest person on Earth. I mean, I could take a picture

00:00:59.933 --> 00:01:03.432
and lose it the next day. And you know, you have every picture.

00:01:03.433 --> 00:01:07.966
You told me the other night, you have 90,000 pictures, right?

00:01:07.967 --> 00:01:11.699
CDs, we were talking about. CDs of pictures, yes.

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:15.799
CDs of pictures, you know. And I can mention, I can have some crazy

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:20.066
idea. I'm sort of a crazy idea guy. And I can have some crazy

00:01:20.067 --> 00:01:23.566
idea, and 10 minutes later, John shows up with a picture.

00:01:23.567 --> 00:01:27.932
You know, I don't know anybody else like that.

00:01:27.933 --> 00:01:35.532
I think really the bottom line here is that we are both

00:01:35.533 --> 00:01:40.299
crazy about structure. I mean, we just have it in our blood to understand

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:44.799
the world through understanding and interpreting structure.

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:49.132
And we shared that immediately. And I had a lot of people

00:01:49.133 --> 00:01:56.032
come to my lab, and some of this have this love for structure

00:01:56.033 --> 00:01:58.666
and understanding of structure, and some don't.

00:01:58.667 --> 00:02:07.066
And so our field attracts those people. And this was the basis,

00:02:07.067 --> 00:02:11.132
really the basic bottom line for John and I working together.

00:02:11.133 --> 00:02:14.499
Yeah, but I want to add one thing, because there's another

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:17.599
fundamental way that we're similar, and that is that we're problem

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:21.099
solvers. And I mean, if I was going to get stranded on a boat

00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:25.399
with no motor, somewhere off at sea, I wanted to be with

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:30.366
Tom. Because he's the only person that I feel we could solve

00:02:30.367 --> 00:02:35.432
the problem of our situation appropriately together. That's why

00:02:35.433 --> 00:02:38.632
it's worked between us. Because we are some complementary in that.

00:02:38.633 --> 00:02:40.699
And how are you different?

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:43.099
Avogadro's number.

00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:52.066
No, no, I mean the point, remember I love to do theoretical calculations.

00:02:52.067 --> 00:02:53.266
Even if they're wrong.

00:02:53.267 --> 00:02:57.866
It's in my blood, though I'm not really precise. And I just want to see how

00:02:57.867 --> 00:03:01.566
the math goes. And you know, I'll throw in any old number

00:03:01.567 --> 00:03:07.732
and run the calculation. And I did. We were trying to figure out

00:03:07.733 --> 00:03:10.866
how many molecules you could put in a vesicle, so I ran the

00:03:10.867 --> 00:03:14.699
calculation. And in order to do that, you need a constant

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:19.632
called Avogadro's number. And so I threw in it in the calculation, later

00:03:19.633 --> 00:03:23.199
on I think you went and talked about or we published it. But

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:26.666
the point was that I had the number wrong by an order of magnitude.

00:03:26.667 --> 00:03:27.799
Four orders of magnitude.

00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:32.132
No, it was a critical calculation. You and I were calculating

00:03:32.133 --> 00:03:35.632
how much tracer we would need to get in a nerve terminal

00:03:35.633 --> 00:03:39.066
in the medium to hope to get a bit of that tracer into these

00:03:39.067 --> 00:03:41.999
little synaptic vesicles. And it was going to be our first evidence

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:45.699
that they actually merged with the cell surface. So we're calculating

00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:49.466
on the blackboard what was the concentration of these little tracers

00:03:49.467 --> 00:03:52.499
that we would need to add. Tom figured out the volume of

00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:55.832
the vesicle, had Avogadro's number wrong by four orders of

00:03:55.833 --> 00:04:01.532
magnitude, so we calculated it would have to be 10 Molar

00:04:01.533 --> 00:04:04.399
HRP or something.

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:07.499
And luckily you got it wrong.

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:10.966
So that worked out.

00:04:10.967 --> 00:04:14.899
The experiment worked.

00:04:14.900 --> 00:04:16.399
Despite us.

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:21.332
So, I'd like to ask you. Why bother collaborating, right?

00:04:21.333 --> 00:04:24.132
You're both smart, you could've done these experiments on your own.

00:04:24.133 --> 00:04:27.199
What was the -- why deal with each other?

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:30.166
That's something that seems like a silly question.

00:04:30.167 --> 00:04:32.932
Like asking why get married? You know, we're not businessmen

00:04:32.933 --> 00:04:39.266
calculating a profit. We feel these things in our heart and we

00:04:39.267 --> 00:04:43.932
-- I felt an attraction to John's way of thinking about stuff and

00:04:43.933 --> 00:04:45.899
it happens. This happens.

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:52.166
Also the issue of as you advance, as you make little observations

00:04:52.167 --> 00:04:55.332
that are even big ones and get excited about your work,

00:04:55.333 --> 00:04:58.466
it's just a whole lot more fun to share it with somebody

00:04:58.467 --> 00:05:01.599
who's totally with you and got the understanding of it.

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:05.899
And is on the same path and is struggling to get it with you.

00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:07.666
As opposed to doing it all by yourself.

00:05:07.667 --> 00:05:12.232
We were together late one night and we sort of asked a

00:05:12.233 --> 00:05:18.166
-- I think you asked you a very great question, you know, why are we

00:05:18.167 --> 00:05:20.532
doing this together? Why are we here doing all this stuff?

00:05:20.533 --> 00:05:23.966
And you said to me, you said the most important thing

00:05:23.967 --> 00:05:30.032
in my life is I want to be -- I want to share discovering something,

00:05:30.033 --> 00:05:35.332
having the truth unfold with somebody. I want to be there

00:05:35.333 --> 00:05:39.466
-- I want to be there when it happens. And you know, we

00:05:39.467 --> 00:05:42.199
were sort of committed to each other at the beginning

00:05:42.200 --> 00:05:43.099
when it happened.

00:05:43.100 --> 00:05:48.432
All scientists want to share their results with a broader community.

00:05:48.433 --> 00:05:52.699
They want to make an impact, make a contribution, and so on.

00:05:52.700 --> 00:05:55.866
But this is a more intimate thing. It's just your day to day

00:05:55.867 --> 00:05:59.766
operation, day to day thinking about science, somehow it's

00:05:59.767 --> 00:06:02.732
just a whole lot more fun to share it with a friend if you can.

00:06:02.733 --> 00:06:03.732
It's just great.

00:06:03.733 --> 00:06:06.766
So the first thing is that it's fun to work with somebody,

00:06:06.767 --> 00:06:11.832
but the second is that there's a potential story that lies beyond just

00:06:11.833 --> 00:06:14.632
having fun with each other. Some insight that you wouldn't have

00:06:14.633 --> 00:06:16.432
if you were working alone.

00:06:16.433 --> 00:06:20.799
Well there's a whole other end. Let's say I might come out to Colorado

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:24.832
because I want to bring some specimens and work with somebody who's

00:06:24.833 --> 00:06:30.566
really good in a mass spec lab. That doesn't even touch the kind of thing we're

00:06:30.567 --> 00:06:33.232
talking about here. Scientists work together sometimes because

00:06:33.233 --> 00:06:37.666
science has become so complex and so specialized that you can

00:06:37.667 --> 00:06:46.266
get things done by working together. And you have to give up

00:06:46.267 --> 00:06:48.766
the kind of, I want to do everything myself, I want the

00:06:48.767 --> 00:06:54.566
the glory. You need to know how to share and the ethics

00:06:54.567 --> 00:06:57.866
of sharing, and the ethics of writing papers together,

00:06:57.867 --> 00:07:01.899
and all that. But that's a different issue.

00:07:01.900 --> 00:07:06.366
Yeah, that's the issue of symbiosis in science. And yes, that's

00:07:06.367 --> 00:07:10.499
a critical ingredient. But this is a more personal thing, it's just a

00:07:10.500 --> 00:07:15.866
lot more fun to do it with a friend, and particularly when

00:07:15.867 --> 00:07:21.499
you've been working together for a long time and had a common

00:07:21.500 --> 00:07:26.633
struggle. A common goal. And it just feels that much sweeter.

